impl-gc

Small JVM-like runtime in Rust.
It loads `.class` files from `sample/`, interprets bytecode, allocates objects in young space,
and promotes survivors into old space during minor GC.

This project is:
`classfile` parser + runtime class model + bytecode interpreter + object allocator + GC plumbing.

This project is not:
a full Java VM.
No full JDK integration, no full verifier, no complete JVM opcode surface.

Integration scope in this repository:
`src/classfile/mod.rs` parses class bytes and resolves constant-pool entries.
`src/interpreter/mod.rs` executes methods and resolves field/method/class refs from constant pool at runtime.
`src/mutator.rs` handles fast allocation path and write barrier.
`src/gc/*` handles mark/sweep/promotion paths when young space fills.
`src/main.rs` drives loading, entrypoint execution, probe output, and GC summary reporting.

Build:
`cargo build`

Run all sample entrypoints:
`cargo run --quiet`

Run one class:
`cargo run --quiet -- GcStressTest`

Enable opcode trace:
`cargo run --quiet -- --trace`

Force visible GC activity:
`IMPL_GC_YOUNG_BYTES=32768 IMPL_GC_OLD_BYTES=8388608 cargo run --quiet`

Expected GC visibility in forced mode:
`[gc] minor: roots=... young_before=... promoted=... freed=...`
and final:
`gc summary: minor=... major=... young_used=... old_used=...`

Why `minor=0 major=0` can still happen:
default young heap is large enough for the sample workload, so allocation never exhausts young space.
No exhaustion means no collection call.

About running Java directly:
`java ./sample/Foo.java` is not the normal class execution form.
Use:
`javac sample/Foo.java`
`java -cp sample Foo`

For this repository you already have compiled sample `.class` files; `cargo run --quiet`
executes them through this runtime.
